Kreative Studio pose editor (STU-PE1). Plain JavaScript, no libraries, no build step. The same file runs in Node 22 (CommonJS require) and in the browser (classic <script>, no modules). Use exactly this wrapper and nothing outside it:

(function () {
  'use strict';
  const NS = typeof module === 'object' && module.exports ? null : (globalThis.KKPose = globalThis.KKPose || {});
  const need = (name, file) => (NS ? NS[name] : require(file));
  // ... the code; get other pose modules with need('vec', './vec.js') INSIDE functions (load order in the browser is free) ...
  const api = { /* exported names */ };
  if (NS) NS.<NAME> = api; else module.exports = api;
})();

Angles are degrees in every API. Coordinates are metres: +X is the figure's left, -Y its front, +Z up. Matrices are arrays of 9 numbers, row-major. Do not change the test file.


Write public/js/pose/skeleton.js (NAME = skeleton), the joint table of the base mesh. Build the tables once, at load time, with `const V = NS ? NS.vec : require('./vec.js');` (the page loads vec.js before this file).

KEEP THE FILE SHORT (under 120 lines): write only the CENTRE and LEFT body rows and the hand table below as compact data, and make the right side and the hands with loops. Never write r_ joints or hand joints out one by one.

Each joint object is {name, parent, rest:[x,y,z], aim, front:[x,y,z], limits:{bend:[lo,hi], side:[lo,hi], twist:[lo,hi]}}. parent = the parent's name (null for pelvis); aim = the child the bone points at, or null for a leaf; front = the reference direction for the bend. A leaf has limits {bend:[0,0], side:[0,0], twist:[0,0]}.

